
@attribute [Route(GlobalValues.Path_Switch_Overview)]

<PageTitle>@GlobalValues.Main_Nav_Section_Heading_Switch @GlobalValues.Page_Title_Switch_Overview_Test</PageTitle>

<section class="@GlobalValues.Info_Box_Class @GlobalValues.Info_Box_Coloured_Modifier">
    <h1 class="@GlobalValues.Info_Box_Heading_Class">Switch Component Overview & Tests</h1>

    <p>
        The Switch component is a simple toggle component that is either on or off communicated to assistive technologies via the use of the "switch" role.
    </p>
    <p>
        <b>Released: </b>Current version <b>v1.0.0</b> See the documentation site for more information: <a target="_blank" class="@GlobalValues.Link_Class" href="@GlobalValues.Site_Link_Docs_Switch">Swtich indicator documentation (opens in new window)</a>
    </p>
</section>

<section class="@GlobalValues.Info_Box_Class">

    <h2 class="@GlobalValues.Info_Box_Heading_Class">Information & instructions</h2>
    <p>
        The tests are straight forward. You should be able to toggle the switch with either your mouse, the space or enter key on your keyboard or via a voice command, using the switches
        accessible name - the visible label to the left of the switch.
    </p>
    <p>
        There are two switches used in the test, one labelled "Airplane mode" which is enabled, the second labelled "Bluetooth" which is aria disabled.
    </p>
    <h3 id="switch-keyboard" class="@GlobalValues.Info_Box_Heading_Class">Keyboard interaction</h3>
    <ul aria-labelledby="switch-keyboard">
        <li><kbd>Space</kbd> - when focus is on the Switch, toggles the state of the switch.</li>
        <li><kbd>Enter</kbd> - when focus is on the Switch, toggles the state of the switch.</li>
    </ul>
</section>

<section class="@GlobalValues.Info_Box_Class">

    <h3 class="@GlobalValues.Info_Box_Heading_Class">Expectations</h3>

    <ul>
        <li>
            Once the Switch has the focus you should be able to activate it with your mouse or keyboard using the keys described. 
        </li>
        <li>
            Voice commands should toggle the switch at any time, whilst enabled.
        </li>
        <li>
            Mouse users can click on either the switch or its label to toggle its state.
        </li>
        <li>
            An aria disabled switch should be announced to screen readers as disabled. Sighted users will get visual conformation that the switch is disabled
            and the usual pointer will change to a not allowed icon. No interaction will be possible, using any method, but the control will remain in the tab order and 
            receive focus.
        </li>
    </ul>
</section>
<section class="section-box" style="width:320px;">
    <Switch AriaDisabled="false" Label="Airplane mode:" @bind-SwitchState="@_airplaneState" SpaceBetween="true" style="margin-bottom:1rem;" />
    <Switch AriaDisabled="true" Label="Bluetooth:" @bind-SwitchState="@_blueToothState" SpaceBetween="true" />
</section>

<div class="flex-content justify-content-between">
    <a class="@GlobalValues.Link_Button_Class @GlobalValues.Link_Button_Left_Arrow_Modifier" href="@GlobalValues.Path_SkipTo_Overview">
        <span aria-hidden="true"></span>
        <span>@GlobalValues.Main_Nav_Section_Heading_SkipTo - @GlobalValues.Page_Title_SkipTo_Overview_Test</span>
        <span aria-hidden="true"></span>
    </a>
    <a class="@GlobalValues.Link_Button_Class @GlobalValues.Link_Button_Right_Arrow_Modifier" href="@GlobalValues.Path_Switch_Quirks">
        <span aria-hidden="true"></span>
        <span>@GlobalValues.Main_Nav_Section_Heading_Switch - @GlobalValues.Page_Title_Switch_Quirks</span>
        <span aria-hidden="true"></span>
    </a>
</div>

@code {
    private bool _airplaneState = false;
    private bool _blueToothState = false;
}